   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law and the education law, in relation to\nthe dispensing of partially filled prescriptions",
    "basePrintNo": "S1813",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S1813-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 42,
       "fullName": "Jen Metzger",
       "imgName": "1054_Jen_Metzger.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 1234,
       "sessionMemberId": 1232,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "METZGER"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §3333, Pub Health L; amd §6816, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S1813\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law and the education law, in relation\nto the dispensing of partially filled prescriptions\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo amend the Public Health Law and the Education Law, in relation to\ndispensing partially filled prescriptions.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends section 3333 of the Public Health Law by\nadding a new subdivision six that allows a prescription for a schedule\nII, III or IV controlled substance to be partially filled at the request\nof the prescriber or patient.\n\nSection two of the bill amends paragraph a of subdivision one of section\n6816 of the Education Law by adding a reference to. section 3333 of the\nPublic Health Law.\n\nSection three of the bill establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn this day and age of the opioid crisis, we are trying to limit the\nsupply of addictive medications made available to patients; however, an\nexisting policy achieves the opposite.\n\nWhen a patient fills a prescription for a controlled substance, they\ncannot request a partial fill unless they are willing to forfeit the\nremainder (except under limited circumstances). Most patients will\naccept the full amount since they are paying the cost of their copay;\nyet, there are some patients who would prefer a partial fill due to\nconcern of the volume of drugs in their household and based on a greater\nawareness of the current opioid crisis. The intent is to allow pharma-\ncists, at the request of patients, to reduce the amount dispensed to\nhelp address the supply in the community.\n\nIn the absence of a Department of Health regulatory change related to\nsupplies of partially filled prescriptions for controlled substances,\nthis bill seeks to limit the supply of the highly addictive drugs to a\npracticable extent. The language in this bill will ensure that New York\nState Law is consistent with the federal law in 21 U.S. Code 829, which\npermits a controlled substance to be partially filled at the request of\nthe patient or prescriber for up to thirty days.\n\nThe statistics surrounding the opioid epidemic are staggering. Despite\nour best efforts to stem the flow of controlled substance prescriptions,\nrecent data from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention shows\nthat the amount of opioids prescribed per person was three times higher\nin 2015 than in 1999. In 2015, six times more opioids per resident were\ndispensed in the highest-prescribing counties than in the lowest-pres-\ncribing counties. Deaths-from opioids have been rising dramatically for\nyears, and there are now nearly 100 deaths a day from opioid overdoses.\n\nThe Internet System for Tracking Over-Prescribing - Prescription Moni-\ntoring Program (ISTOP/PMP) has been a meaningful tool in this fight, but\nwe must look at addressing this problem with a multi-faceted approach.\nThough New York has been a leader in enacting progressive policies aimed\nat addressing this problem, we are still one of the nation's leaders in\nopioid-related deaths.\n\nNew York regulation 10 NYCRR 80.73(j) does not permit supply of the\nremainder of a partially filled prescription for a schedule II\ncontrolled substance, benzodiazepine, or other medications listed under\n80.67 (a) except when unable to supply, the patient is terminally ill,\nor the patient is a resident of a residential health care facility.\n\nThe Pharmacists Society of the State of New York (PSSNY) adopted a\nresolution in January of 2017 advocating for this change to the regu-\nlation regarding partially filled prescriptions. This resolution is a\nclear message from pharmacists offering a solution to the problem, and\nthe intent is to allow pharmacists, at a patient's request, to reduce\nthe amount dispensed in an effort to address the supply in the communi-\nty.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the first of January next succeeding the\ndate upon which it shall have become a law. Effective immediately the\naddition, amendment and/or repeal of any rule or regulation necessary\nfor the implementation of this act on its effective date are authorized\nto he made on or before such date.\n",
